WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.125
The CFR railway workshops were state-owned
– they belonged to the state, the

2
00:00:06.175 --> 00:00:09.050
capitalist state of the early 1900s,

3
00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:13.409
from after the war right up until '45.

4
00:00:13.459 --> 00:00:16.850
So they belonged to the state.

5
00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:21.744
The country was going through a
crisis, the crisis of 1929 to 1933.

6
00:00:21.794 --> 00:00:27.150
They were asked to accept a 'sacrifice
curve' – that's what it was called.

7
00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:34.050
It was asked of them, but only of them, mind
you – the people at the top weren't touched.

8
00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:38.969
Nothing was cut for them, especially
not for the judges – there I go making

9
00:00:39.019 --> 00:00:40.150
comparisons again.

10
00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:45.940
Actually, it's not me
making them, it's history.

11
00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:51.850
But their wages really
were cut very drastically.

12
00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:55.850
There were two very powerful unions there:

13
00:00:55.900 --> 00:01:05.056
the locomotive drivers' union and the union
of the other railway workers, the ones

14
00:01:05.106 --> 00:01:07.750
who repaired the trains,

15
00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:15.250
because these were CFR repair workshops:
carriages, rolling stock and so on.

16
00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:21.026
There were around 20,000 workers there.

17
00:01:21.076 --> 00:01:26.950
So it was huge. You saw it on our walk –

18
00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:34.450
they stretch from near Piața Chibrit, near
Mureș, almost all the way to the Grant Bridge.

19
00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:40.550
They ran along the railway, including the spot
where the MedLife clinic is now, and so on.

20
00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:44.990
The workers were organised in unions.

21
00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:48.350
It wasn't a political thing.

22
00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:51.750
That's where history lied.

23
00:01:51.800 --> 00:02:00.650
The reason they rose up
was social and economic

24
00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:04.318
and, I'll say it again, about the unions.

25
00:02:04.368 --> 00:02:10.850
The movement was led by the union
leaders, headed by a man called Doncea,

26
00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:16.850
who later joined the Party – but it wasn't
led by the communists, as they claimed.

27
00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:20.050
The communists made the
most of the opportunity too.

28
00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:22.450
There were a few of them, but very few,

29
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.750
because the Communist Party had been outlawed
at the time, since 1924, under the Mârzescu Law.

30
00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:35.839
And being outlawed, any kind
of demonstration meant arrest.

31
00:02:35.889 --> 00:02:37.650
No question about it.

32
00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:45.299
They were against the country, essentially,
because they took their orders from

33
00:02:45.349 --> 00:02:49.850
Moscow, from the Soviet
Union, founded in 1917,

34
00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:54.750
and they campaigned for Greater
Romania to be broken up.

35
00:02:54.800 --> 00:03:00.574
Let's be clear: that was the policy of the
Romanian Communist Party from the 1920s

36
00:03:00.624 --> 00:03:02.350
and '30s right up to '44.

37
00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:05.650
Greater Romania had only just
come into being, in 1918,

38
00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:12.150
and Moscow – the Soviet Union
of Lenin and then Stalin –

39
00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:18.250
didn't want Bessarabia to be ours, but
theirs – as it still is today, sadly.

40
00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:25.150
So. That's what the communists were up to.

41
00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:30.450
That's why they were banned and went
underground: they were against the country.

42
00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:39.450
Because of these major problems, the workers
tried to negotiate with the management –

43
00:03:39.500 --> 00:03:45.250
which, I'll say it again, was the state's
– with the engineers, with the directors,

44
00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:50.750
with the management, who made them
promises but didn't sort out a thing.

45
00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:57.550
And they called in the
army and the gendarmerie,

46
00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:03.350
which came under the Bucharest
Prefecture at the time.

47
00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:14.636
The gendarmes and soldiers who went in
were commanded by, among others, the

48
00:04:14.686 --> 00:04:18.850
notorious Gavrilă Marinescu,

49
00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:23.850
who was a big boss at the
Prefecture, and by Armand Călinescu,

50
00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:29.250
who later went on to become prime minister.

51
00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:35.350
He was Interior Minister at the time –
the one who was assassinated in 1939.

52
00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:42.350
So the workers had occupied the
workshops the evening before –

53
00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:49.550
on the 14th, I think – refused to work
any more and barricaded themselves inside.

54
00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:59.150
The gendarmerie and the authorities
ordered them to stop. They refused,

55
00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:04.974
because they hadn't got what they'd been
promised; on top of that, some of their

56
00:05:05.024 --> 00:05:09.050
leaders were arrested on the
night of 15 to 16 February.

57
00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:20.050
And on the morning of 16 February, at six –
just after six, it's hard to say exactly –

58
00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:28.150
right after the first shift had come in
– the third shift was already there, they

59
00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:30.250
worked three shifts –

60
00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:34.250
they stormed in.

61
00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:39.150
Shots were fired. Six or seven people died.

62
00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:45.950
Many died not from the shooting but in the
crush that followed: they fell into the pits

63
00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:54.543
that were there for working under the
locomotives – pits dug out so you could get

64
00:05:54.593 --> 00:05:58.150
underneath an engine to repair it.

65
00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:03.050
A lot of people fell in there;
a great many were injured.

66
00:06:03.100 --> 00:06:05.887
My father – what happened to him?

67
00:06:05.937 --> 00:06:12.850
He'd come in that morning just after six
for his shift; he worked the same hours.

68
00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:20.043
And one of his bosses, an older hand, when
he heard the warning shots, said to him –

69
00:06:20.093 --> 00:06:27.150
my father told me every detail – 'Come on,
let's get out of here, this is serious!'

70
00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:30.780
He knew – he'd been through
this kind of thing before.

71
00:06:30.830 --> 00:06:35.350
But how could they get out, when
there was no way through the front?

72
00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:40.110
They jumped over the fence by the railway
and ran along the tracks as far as

73
00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:41.550
Chibrit, Piața Chibrit.

74
00:06:41.600 --> 00:06:45.116
They caught the tram and each went home.

75
00:06:45.166 --> 00:06:48.950
My father lived in Parcul Rahova back then.

76
00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:51.650
That's how he got away.

77
00:06:51.700 --> 00:07:01.350
Why do I say he got away –
alive, unhurt, and a free man?

78
00:07:01.400 --> 00:07:08.867
Because the people who stormed in – the
authorities, the gendarmerie, the army; as

79
00:07:08.917 --> 00:07:16.750
far as I know it wasn't a police matter, it
was down to the gendarmerie and the army –

80
00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:23.650
under the laws of the day,
rounded up absolutely everyone.

81
00:07:23.700 --> 00:07:27.599
They took away several thousand people.

82
00:07:27.649 --> 00:07:31.650
The injured were taken off to hospitals,

83
00:07:31.700 --> 00:07:34.764
and everyone else was taken to Malmaison.

84
00:07:34.814 --> 00:07:41.750
Malmaison was on Calea Plevnei – a sort of
holding prison for the capital, a sorting centre.

85
00:07:41.800 --> 00:07:47.552
That's where they sorted them. Some were
let go after a few days, but they spent a

86
00:07:47.602 --> 00:07:49.250
good few days locked up.

87
00:07:49.300 --> 00:07:53.750
And some they kept for good:
the ones who were Party members.

88
00:07:53.800 --> 00:08:02.050
And that's where Gheorghiu-Dej came in –
he tried to latch on to the movement too –

89
00:08:02.100 --> 00:08:09.850
Chivu Stoica, some Petrescu or other –
Dumitru Petrescu, I think – and others.

90
00:08:09.900 --> 00:08:20.250
All the Communist Party members were arrested,
and they stayed locked up for 11 years,

91
00:08:20.300 --> 00:08:25.190
until 1944, until 23 August. They
were only released on 23 August.

